    unit_outage_dispatched_bin_denominator: { cell: "U", ev: "Row added by miso-266 (2026-09-22) with the field, same PR (rule 28c). UNTESTED here (rule 28(d)): no MISO verdict crosses the boundary (rule 25 [R-ISO-SCOPE]), and the SIZE of the defect is a per-ISO FLEET property \u2014 how far this ISO's dispatched (plant_code, plant_group) capacity departs from outages._iso_plant_capacity's reconstruction of it. THE TRANSFER QUESTION IS ZERO-LP AND WELL-POSED, and is MISO's, reusable verbatim (scripts/probes/_miso266_denominator_vs_lp.py): rebuild this ISO's keeper fleet fleet_only for each registered year, sum pmax per (plant_code, plant_group), and set it beside _iso_plant_capacity(iso). Read three numbers \u2014 (i) how many bins carry denom/cap_LP below 1 (those OVER-remove, and a bin whose flagged units then sum past the clip is zeroed while running), (ii) how many bins the LP dispatches that the reconstructed map LACKS ENTIRELY (those ride un-derated through their own measured outage \u2014 the SPP-48 Oklaunion pathology), and (iii) whether this ISO synthesizes exit-cohort bins at all (fleet/assembly.py's _r{yyyy}{mm} tag fires only where the bins sheet carries Retirement_Year, so an ISO with no mid-window partial-plant exits may find the two maps already agree and the flag inert). An ISO arming unit_outage_lp_capacity_basis or unit_outage_extract_basis_share must disarm it first \u2014 the loaders raise, by rule 19 [R-ONE-MECH]. NOTE THE SCOPE before reading any census: the CHP groups (CC_CHP / CT_CHP / ST_CHP) are EXCLUDED by construction \u2014 there cap_LP is the grid-facing residual after the behind-the-meter host steam is held out, so the incumbent nameplate denominator is the correct one and the flag is the identity. A CHP-heavy ratio in this ISO's census is therefore NOT this flag's object." },
